.TH DC 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
dc \- reverse-Polish arbitrary-precision calculator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dc
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR script ]...
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]...
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dc
runs commands written in reverse-Polish notation on a stack of
arbitrary-precision decimal numbers and strings.
Numbers and strings are pushed on the stack, and most other commands pop
their operands and push their results.
Only the print commands write to standard output.
.PP
This is the busybox
.B dc
applet, which shares its number code with
.BR bc (1).
It accepts the GNU extensions
.B ~
and
.BR | ,
the FreeBSD extensions
.BR ( ,
.BR { ,
.BR G ,
and
.BR N ,
and, with
.BR \-x ,
extended register names.
Some commands behave unexpectedly; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
.B dc
runs each
.B \-e
script and
.B \-f
file in the order given, then each
.I file
operand.
With none of these, or with only
.BR \-x ,
it reads standard input instead.
A
.I file
named
.B \-
is a file of that name; standard input is
.IR /dev/stdin .
The stack, the registers, and the scale and bases carry over from one
input to the next.
.B dc
reads its input a line at a time, and runs each command as soon as it
has read the start of the next one.
It stops at the end of the last input or when a quit command exits.
.PP
When standard input is a console,
.B dc
is interactive.
It prints no banner and no prompt, and reads lines with line editing and
history as in
.BR sh (1),
but with no completion.
After an error in a typed line,
.B dc
discards the rest of the line and the stack, keeps the registers, and
reads the next line.
Ctrl-C at the prompt ends
.BR dc ,
as does Ctrl-D on an empty line.
Ctrl-C while a typed command is running stops it, prints
.BR "interrupted execution" ,
and reads the next line.
.PP
A console on standard input also changes how scripts and files run.
An error in a
.I file
operand clears the stack and skips the rest of that file, and
.B dc
goes on with the next one.
An error in a
.B \-e
script or a
.B \-f
file still ends
.B dc
with status 1.
Ctrl-C while a script or file runs ends
.BR dc .
.PP
When standard input is not a console, the first error ends
.B dc
with status 1.
.SS Syntax
Whitespace may separate any two commands, and is needed only where two
numbers would otherwise run together, and before an extended register
name.
A
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
.PP
A number is a run of the digits
.B 0
to
.B 9
and
.B A
to
.BR F ,
with at most one decimal point.
The letters must be uppercase, since lowercase letters are commands.
A second decimal point starts a new number, so
.B 1.2.3
is
.B 1.2
followed by
.BR .3 .
A backslash directly followed by a newline inside a number is skipped,
so
.B dc
reads back the long numbers it prints.
A number starts with
.B _
to be negative, as in
.BR _5 ;
.B \-
is only the subtraction command.
.PP
A digit counts as its own value when it is the only digit after any
leading zeros, so
.B A
and
.B 0A
are 10 in any input base.
In a longer number, a digit not less than the input base counts as one
less than the base, so with input base 10,
.B FF
is 99.
.PP
A number is converted from its digits the first time a command uses it,
not when it is pushed.
A change of input base therefore changes the value of numbers pushed
earlier that no command has used yet, so
.B FF 16 i p
prints 255, not 99.
.PP
A string is the text between
.B [
and
.BR ] ,
which may span lines and may contain nested pairs of brackets.
It has no escape sequences, so it cannot contain an unbalanced bracket.
A string is also a macro, which
.B x
and the conditional commands run as commands.
.PP
Any character that is not whitespace, a digit, part of a string, or a
command is an error.
.SS Registers
The commands
.BR s ,
.BR l ,
.BR S ,
.BR L ,
.BR : ,
.BR ; ,
and the conditionals are followed by a register name.
The name is a single character, any character, even a space or a digit:
in
.BR "s a" ,
the register is named by the space, and
.B a
is a command.
.PP
With
.BR \-x ,
a command that is followed by whitespace other than a newline skips it
and takes the following run of lowercase letters, digits, and
underscores as the name,
as in
.B "s total"
and
.BR "l total" .
A command followed directly by a character still takes that character,
so with
.BR \-x ,
.B sa
is the register
.BR a .
.PP
A register holds a stack of values, each a number or a string.
A register that was never set holds the number 0.
Arrays are separate from registers, so an array and a register may share
a name.
.SS Arithmetic
The binary commands pop the top number as
.I b
and the next as
.IR a ,
and push the result of
.I a
operated on by
.IR b .
.TP
.B +
Push the sum.
.TP
.B \-
Push the difference,
.I a
minus
.IR b .
.TP
.B *
Push the product.
.TP
.B /
Push the quotient.
.TP
.B %
Push the remainder,
.I a
minus the product of
.I b
and the quotient from
.BR / .
.TP
.B ~
Push the quotient, then the remainder, so that the remainder is on top.
.TP
.B ^
Push
.I a
raised to the power
.IR b .
.I b
must be an integer; a fractional part is accepted only if all its digits
are zero.
A negative
.I b
gives the reciprocal of the power.
.TP
.B |
Pop three numbers,
.IR a ,
.IR b ,
and the modulus
.I m
on top, and push
.I a
raised to
.I b
modulo
.IR m .
All three must be integers with a scale of 0, so
.B 2.0
is rejected.
The exponent must not be negative, and the modulus must not be zero.
.TP
.B v
Pop one number and push its square root.
A negative number is an error.
.PP
Results are truncated, not rounded.
The scale, set by
.BR k ,
is the number of digits after the point that
.B /
and
.BR ~ ,
and a negative power, keep.
A remainder, as from
.B %
or
.BR ~ ,
keeps the larger of the scale plus the scale of
.I b
and the scale of
.IR a .
Sums and differences keep every digit.
A product keeps the smaller of the sum of the operands' scales and the
largest of the scale and the operands' scales.
A power with a non-negative exponent
.I n
keeps the smaller of
.I n
times the scale of
.I a
and the larger of the scale and the scale of
.IR a .
A square root keeps the larger of the scale and the scale of its
operand.
.SS Stack
.TP
.B c
Empty the stack.
.TP
.B d
Push a copy of the top value.
.TP
.B r
Swap the top two values.
.TP
.B R
Pop and discard the top value.
.TP
.B z
Push the number of values on the stack, counted before the push.
.SS "Registers and arrays"
.TP
.BI s r
Pop a value and store it in register
.IR r ,
replacing the register's top value.
.TP
.BI l r
Push a copy of register
.IR r 's
top value.
.TP
.BI S r
Pop a value and push it on register
.IR r 's
own stack.
A string is pushed there but also stays on the main stack.
.TP
.BI L r
Pop the top value of register
.IR r 's
stack and push it on the main stack.
A register's stack never becomes empty, so it is an error unless
.B S
has pushed more values on the register than
.B L
has popped.
.TP
.BI : r
Pop an index
.I i
and a value, and store the value as element
.I i
of array
.IR r .
The value must be a number.
.TP
.BI ; r
Pop an index
.I i
and push element
.I i
of array
.IR r .
Elements that were never stored are 0.
What is pushed refers to the element instead of holding its value, so
a later store to that element changes it.
.PP
An index is a non-negative integer no greater than 2147483647.
Any fractional part is dropped.
.SS "Macros and conditionals"
.TP
.B x
Pop a value.
If it is a string, run it as commands.
A number is left on the stack and nothing runs.
.TP
\fB<\fIr\fR, \fB>\fIr\fR, \fB=\fIr\fR, \fB!<\fIr\fR, \fB!>\fIr\fR, \fB!=\fIr\fR
Pop two numbers, and run the string in register
.I r
as a macro if the top number compares to the second as the operator says:
less, greater, equal, not less, not greater, or not equal.
For example,
.B 3 5 >a
runs register
.B a
because 5 is greater than 3.
The register is not examined when the test fails.
It is an error if it is run and its top value is not a string.
.IP
The register may be followed by
.B e
and a second register, as in
.BR "3 5 >a eb" ,
whose macro runs when the test fails.
The
.B e
must be on the same line, though whitespace may come before it.
.TP
.B (
Pop two numbers and push 1 if the top is less than the second, else 0.
.TP
.B {
Pop two numbers and push 1 if the top is less than or equal to the
second, else 0.
.TP
.B G
Pop two numbers and push 1 if they are equal, else 0.
.TP
.B N
Pop one number and push 1 if it is zero, else 0.
.TP
.B ?\&
Read a line from standard input and run it as commands.
When the script itself is standard input, that is the next line of the
script.
At the end of input it runs nothing.
.TP
.B q
Exit
.B dc
if at most one macro is running.
Otherwise stop the two innermost running macros.
.TP
.B Q
Pop a number
.I n
and stop the
.I n
innermost running macros.
If
.I n
is one more than the number running,
.B dc
exits.
A larger
.I n
is an error, and 0 does nothing.
.SS "Scale and bases"
.TP
.B k
Pop a number and make it the scale, from 0 to 4294967295.
The default is 0.
.TP
.B K
Push the scale.
.TP
.B i
Pop a number and make it the input base, from 2 to 36.
The default is 10.
.TP
.B I
Push the input base.
.TP
.B o
Pop a number and make it the output base, from 2 to 999.
The default is 10.
.TP
.B O
Push the output base.
.PP
These commands drop any fractional part of the number.
.SS Printing
A number is printed in the output base, with a leading
.B \-
if negative and with no zero before the point.
With an output base above 16, each digit is a decimal number padded with
zeros to the width of the largest digit, and preceded by a space, or by
the point for the first digit after it.
Output bases 16 and below use the digits
.B 0
to
.B 9
and
.B A
to
.BR F .
.TP
.B p
Print the top value followed by LF, without popping it.
In a string, the sequences
.BR \en ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \er ,
and
.B \et
stand for the control characters, and
.B \ee
and
.B \e\e
for a single backslash.
A backslash before any other character is printed with it.
An empty string prints a NUL byte before the LF.
.TP
.B n
The same as
.BR p .
.TP
.B f
Print every value from the top down, as
.B p
does.
.TP
.B P
Print the top value with no LF, without popping it.
A string is written as it is, without translating backslashes, and an
empty string writes nothing.
A number is written as the bytes of its integer part in base 256, most
significant first, followed by its fractional part as further base 256
digits; 0 is one NUL byte.
A negative number is an error.
.TP
.B a
Pop a value and push a one-byte string: for a number, the remainder of
its integer part divided by 256, which must not be negative, and for a
string, its first byte.
A zero byte gives an empty string instead.
.TP
.B Z
Pop a value and push its length: for a number, its count of digits,
not counting zeros before the first nonzero digit and at least 1, and
for a string, its length in bytes.
.TP
.B X
Pop a number and push its scale, the count of digits after the point.
A string is an error.
.SS Errors
An error is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dc: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
or, for input from a
.B \-f
file or a
.I file
operand, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dc: \fIfile\fR:\fIline\fR: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I line
counts from 1.
A message may end with
.BI "at '" text '
for the rest of the line from the faulty command.
A file that cannot be opened is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
dc: can\(aqt open \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
The other messages are as follows.
.TP
.B stack has too few elements
A command needs more values than the stack holds.
.TP
.B variable is wrong type
A command was given a string where it needs a number, or the reverse,
or a string was stored in an array.
.TP
.BI "bad character '" c '
The character is not a command.
.TP
.B bad token
A command is in the wrong place, such as an
.B e
with no conditional before it, or a
.B _
that is not followed by a number.
.TP
.B unterminated string
A
.B [
has no closing
.BR ] .
.TP
.B divide by zero
The divisor of
.BR / ,
.BR % ,
or
.BR ~ ,
or the modulus of
.BR | ,
is zero.
.TP
.B not an integer
The exponent of
.B ^
has a nonzero fractional part, or an operand of
.B |
has a scale other than 0.
.TP
.B negative number
A negative value where one is not allowed: the square root, the
exponent of
.BR | ,
the values for
.BR k ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
and
.BR Q ,
an array index, and the operands of
.B a
and
.BR P .
.TP
.B overflow
A value is too large for 32 bits, as for
.BR k ,
.BR Q ,
or the exponent of
.BR ^ .
.TP
.B array too long; must be [1,2147483647]
An array index is too large.
.TP
.B bad ibase; must be [2,16]
The value for
.B i
is out of range.
The upper limit is 36, not 16 as the message says.
.TP
.B bad obase; must be [2,999]
The value for
.B o
is out of range.
.TP
.BI "illegal character 0x" NN
The input has a byte that is not text; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BI "file '" name "' is not text"
With a console on standard input,
.B dc
reports each such byte on a line of a file, then ends with this message.
.TP
.BR "input error" ", " "output error"
Reading or writing failed, for the reason that follows.
.TP
.B out of memory
Memory ran out, as a very large array index does.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-xe ,
and an option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-e1p ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and so does the first operand, so in
.B dc a.dc \-x
the
.B \-x
is a file name.
.B dc
runs each
.B \-e
and
.B \-f
as soon as it reads it.
.TP
.B \-x
Allow extended register names; see
.BR Registers .
It applies to the scripts and files after it.
.TP
.BI "\-e " script
Run
.I script
as commands.
It may contain newlines.
A command cannot continue from one script to the next.
.TP
.BI "\-f " file
Run the commands in
.IR file .
An error opening it ends
.B dc
with status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime replaces each argument containing wildcards with the names
of the files it matches, if any, even when the argument is quoted.
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
a script argument such as
.B *
may become a list of files.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for its child processes, so commands run from the busybox shell, which
expands wildcards itself, receive their arguments as given.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The console width for line editing, instead of the console's actual
width.
.TP
.B DC_LINE_LENGTH
A printed number is broken into lines of at most this many characters,
each but the last ending in a backslash.
The default is 70, so a full line has 69 digits and a backslash.
The value 0, or a value over 2147483647, disables breaking.
The value 1, a value over 4294967295, and anything that is not an
unsigned decimal number select the default.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input ended, or a quit command ran.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a file that cannot be opened, an error in the
commands, input that is not text, a read or write error, or running out
of memory.
.PP
With a console on standard input, errors in the commands of typed lines,
or of a
.I file
operand other than the last, do not change the status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Evaluate an expression:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq2 3 + 4 * p\(aq
20
.EE
.RE
.PP
Divide to 10 decimal places:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq10k 22 7 / p\(aq
3.1428571428
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read commands from a pipe:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aq72 9 / 8 * p\(aq | dc
64
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert 255 to hexadecimal, and back:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq16 o 255 p\(aq
FF
$ dc \-e \(aq16 i FF p\(aq
255
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compute 20! with a recursive macro in register
.BR f :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq[d1\-d1<f*]sf 20 lfx p\(aq
2432902008176640000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a script file that counts from 1 to 5:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat >count.dc <<\(aqEOF\(aq
# Print 1 to 5.
[li p li 1 + si li 6 >a]sa
1 si lax
EOF
$ dc count.dc
1
2
3
4
5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Say whether 7 is even or odd:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq[[even]p]sa [[odd]p]sb 7 2 % 0 =a eb\(aq
odd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use a long register name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-x \-e \(aq5 s count l count p\(aq
5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compute 4 to the 13th power modulo 497:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq4 13 497 | p\(aq
445
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print a string followed by an LF (10):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dc \-e \(aq[hello, world]P 10P\(aq
hello, world
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Behavior that may surprise a user of another dc:
.IP \(bu 2
.B n
is the same as
.BR p :
it prints a newline and leaves the value on the stack, where the usual
command pops the value and prints no newline.
.IP \(bu 2
.B p
and
.B f
translate backslash sequences in a string and print an empty string as
a NUL byte.
.B P
does neither.
.IP \(bu 2
.B P
does not pop the value, rejects a negative number, and writes the
fractional part of a number.
.IP \(bu 2
.B S
leaves a string on the main stack as well.
.IP \(bu 2
Arrays hold numbers only, and the value that
.B ;
pushes follows later stores to its element.
.IP \(bu 2
A register's stack never becomes empty, and
.B L
cannot pop its last value, so
.B La
after
.B 5 sa
is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
The input base may be up to 36, though the error message says 16, and
only the digits
.B 0
to
.B F
can be written.
.IP \(bu 2
Strings have no escapes, and FreeBSD's backslash escapes for brackets
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no shell escape
.RB ( ! ),
and the FreeBSD commands
.B J
and
.B M
are not implemented.
.PP
Scale, exponents, and the count for
.B Q
are 32-bit values on Windows, so the largest is 4294967295.
An array takes memory up to its largest index, so a large index can
exhaust memory.
A macro that ends by calling itself is not turned into a loop: every call
level uses memory until the macro returns.
.PP
Files and standard input must be ASCII text.
Any byte other than tab, carriage return, newline, and printable ASCII
is an error, including UTF-8 in comments and strings, except that NUL
bytes are ignored.
A script given with
.B \-e
is not checked.
A carriage return is whitespace, so CRLF line endings work, except after
a backslash that continues a number.
A carriage return inside a string stays in it.
Output lines end with LF, and
.B P
writes bytes unchanged to a file or pipe.
.PP
The characters that
.B p
prints for strings count toward the line length of the next number
printed, because the LF that
.B p
adds after a string does not restart the count.
That number is therefore broken early, or not at all once the count
reaches the line length.
.B P
does not count the characters of a string.
.PP
Interactive mode requires a Windows console.
In a terminal that connects programs through pipes, such as mintty,
.B dc
behaves as with redirected input: no line editing, and the first error
ends it.
The history holds up to 16384 lines and is not saved.
.PP
.I file
operands may use drive letters and backslashes, and messages show them
as given.
The names
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/fd/N ,
and
.I /dev/null
work as on Unix.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bc (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1)
